author_feedback_text:
We thank the reviewers for recognizing our work as “clinically relevant” (R2), “promising” (R3), and “novel” (R4). We address key concerns below:
@R2-Q1, R4-Q1/2/3/4 LLM usage and implementation details: For LLM-based label extraction, Qwen3 is used in a constrained manner: mapping short diagnostic conclusions to predefined 0/1 taxonomy labels in a strict JSON format rather than open-ended generation. For label reliability, a neurosurgeon with >3 years of experience reviewed 100 random cases by comparing the LLM-extracted labels with the original reports, yielding 98% exact-match accuracy. For reproducibility, we will release the prompt, code upon acceptance, and report inference statistics (79.4 ms/sample latency; 1003.6 MB peak GPU memory).
@R3-Q1/2 Adapter/transfer scope: We clarify that Brain-Adapter focuses on adapting 2D VLM representations to 3D CT diagnosis via slice aggregation and dual-stream learning. Here, “adapter” denotes a dimensional and domain adaptation strategy (2D-to-3D), rather than indicating a universal PEFT module. We will highlight this definition in the revision to avoid confusion. As a preliminary study, we tested whether PEFT/VLM variants could explain the gain under the same ABMIL setting. BiomedCLIP+VPT/CLIP-Adapter scored 0.863/0.869 Micro-AUC, while our framework reached 0.887; with RadCLIP (Lu et al., TNNLS 2025), VPT/CLIP-Adapter scored 0.857/0.850, while our framework reached 0.879, supporting the advantage of our specific dual-stream design. We also appreciate the reviewer’s suggestion that turning the framework into a more modular adapter component could improve practical use, which we will explore in future work, including compatibility with alternative pre-trained models.
@R2-Q3, R3-Q2 Stronger 3D alternatives: Our work focuses on adapting existing 2D medical VLMs to 3D-aware understanding, rather than exhaustively benchmarking native 3D architectures. As a preliminary check, the recent 3D head FM, HLIP (Zhao et al., TMLR 2026), yielded 0.857 Micro-AUC vs. our 0.887.This suggests that direct 3D modeling of thick-slice CTs may dilute subtle slice-level cues, whereas 2D VLM+MIL preserves and aggregates them effectively.
@R2-Q6, R3-Q3 Interpretability evaluation (R2,R3): We thank the reviewer for highlighting the need for quantitative localization evidence. Our explainability analysis demonstrates that attention concentrates on informative middle slices, suppressing blank z-axis extremities. As a quantitative localization check, a neurosurgeon inspected 100 samples; the model achieved an 87% Top-5 accuracy in locating the manually identified key slice (out of 32 slices). This supports our slice-level localization.
@R2-Q2 Role of report text in model training: We clarify that report-derived labels and report text guidance are separated in our framework. First, logic-set labels supervise the visual-only MIL branch, while text embeddings are excluded from the classifier. Second, TCA uses text only to guide visual slice pooling during training, not to predict labels. Third, the consistency loss is designed to distill this text-guided prior into the visual branch, as supported by our ablation study. In summary, inference remains strictly CT-only, and text serves as training-time semantic guidance for evidence aggregation rather than a shortcut.
@R2-Q4 Pretrained vs. from-scratch behavior: Table 1 shows that pretraining consistently improves threshold-free metrics such as AUC/Hamming loss, while fixed thresholds may shift SEN/SPE in imbalanced multi-label prediction. We will clarify this in the revision.
@R2 R3 Manuscript clarifications: We will revise Table 2 analysis to avoid monotonic-gain claims (R2-Q5), define the taxonomy as a clinically oriented report-derived label space covering both imaging findings and diagnostic context (R2-Q7), and replace unsupported scalability claims with a narrower annotation-efficient formulation (R3-Q4).

