author_feedback_text:
We thank the reviewers for recognizing the “strong contribution” (R1), “large-scale corpus” (R2), and “novel formulation” (R3). We address key concerns below.
@R2-Q1, R3-Q1 Positioning/novelty. First, regarding baselines: current endoscopy FMs (e.g., EndoFM, EndoMamba) remain largely visual self-supervised methods, and the evaluated VLM baselines are general/biomedical models transferred to our corpus, not prior endoscopy-domain VLMs. Our goal is therefore to build a dedicated endoscopy-specific vision-language foundation model. Rather than simply combining existing components, we introduce targeted adaptations for the endoscopic setting of mapping one report to many redundant frames: PSAA progressively unifies anatomy/pathology supervision in a shared latent space via soft targets, AGSP provides the annotation-free local visual units required for this alignment, and SC-MAE reuses AGSP-selected semantic-rich frames for additional visual supervision. This distinguishes EndoVLM from prior uni-modal endoscopy FMs and generic medical VLP settings.
@R2-Q2, R3-Q3 PSAA soft target. We clarify that PSAA uses text similarity not as a proxy for visual similarity, but as a soft relational prior to avoid treating semantically compatible texts as equally hard negatives when shaping the visual-text similarity distribution: if t_k is semantically close to paired text t_u under the same anatomy/pathology constraints, penalizing (v_u,t_k) as a hard negative would introduce false negatives. Moreover, we use text-text rather than visual-visual similarity because endoscopic visual similarity is unstable and easily confounded by acquisition factors, while the PubMedBERT-initialized text space provides a more stable semantic anchor. Clinically, PSAA is designed to impose anatomy- and pathology-aware structure more relevant to downstream diagnosis, rather than to learn a purely visual fine-grained similarity space. This also motivates the asymmetric design: normal samples within the same anatomy share stable healthy patterns, whereas abnormal samples are more heterogeneous, making hard labels overly restrictive. Consistently, a preliminary LIMUC comparison showed that hard-label, same-anatomy uniform, and same-anatomy+pathology uniform targets yielded AUCs of 93.8/94.1/94.2, all below full PSAA (94.5).
@R2-Q3 Zero-shot imbalance on ileum. The lower ileum result is better understood as a category-specific limitation: colonoscopy reports often mention ileum procedurally, e.g., inserting into the terminal ileum, rather than as a consistently described anatomical region, making supervision rarer and less uniformly defined. We will clarify this limitation.
@R2-Q4 Pooling K choices. As a preliminary sensitivity check for AGSP, LIMUC AUCs at K={1,3,5,7,10,20,all-attn} are 94.2/94.5/94.0/94.1/94.2/93.9/94.1, supporting K=3 while suggesting limited sensitivity.
@R2 Presentation/ethics. We will streamline overloaded notations, clearly define index sets, and make Fig. 1 self-contained. Pretraining data were de-identified with ethics approval; details will be disclosed upon acceptance.
@R1 Experimental clarification. We chose ViT-B/16 as a standard backbone comparable to baselines, and PubMedBERT for stable biomedical semantics. For segmentation, our claim is transferability: EndoVLM features with a minimal decoder (1x Conv) achieve competitive performance against engineered task-specific models. “Seen/unseen” only denotes downstream fine-tuning visibility; all Table 1 datasets are disjoint from our private pretraining corpus. Qualitative samples will be added to our repository.
@R3-Q2 Qwen3 parsing pipeline. This is a constrained extraction task over predefined fields, not open-ended generation. As part of dataset quality control, three experienced endoscopists audited 1000 random LLM-extracted sub-reports, yielding 99.8%/99.5% accuracy for anatomy/pathology states. We will clarify the parsing details and note residual extraction noise as a limitation.

