reviews.text_strengths:
Theoretical grounded claims towards attacking VAEs limitations:
The authors properly explores, discusses and provide references for the reader to better understand VAE limitations, as: chance of raising an incoherente global latent geometry (regions with roles or not sufficient represented); lack of interpretability of the learned latent representation; confusion of healthy variations given demographic factors (. e. g, age and sex) with patologies;
Proposer use of literature to tackle VAEs limitations:
Authors adapt to their VAE techniques as SigREG (recently proposed in the LeJEPA article), L0 regularization to select important and interpretable latent dimensions, and an uncertainty-aware W-score based on epistemic uncertainty. The use of SigREG together with L0 regularization enables the authors to enable reliable distance-based deviation measures;
Interpretability:
By individually disturbing each W-score, the authors also enable the visualization of the anatomical subsystem that the W-score represents;
Robust validation:
Authors employ strategies to evaluate in- and out-of-distribution test sets, isolating the effect of testing in samples of the same test set or scanner. They also isolate the effects of age and sex on evaluation, which is also positive, as they employ conditioning with age and sex. Lastly, W-score Q-Q plots show that the learned scores follow a normal distribution centered at 0 and with close to unit variance.
—— MORE DETAILS —-
**Theoretically grounded motivation. ** The authors provide a well-structured and referenced critique of standard VAE-based normative models, identifying three concrete limitations: the risk of an incoherent global latent geometry with redundant or sparsely populated (hole-prone) regions; limited interpretability of the learned latent representation; and the conflation of healthy demographic variation (age and sex) with pathological deviations. This framing effectively justifies each of the proposed contributions;
**Principled use of recent literature to address VAE limitations. ** The authors adapt three complementary techniques to directly tackle the identified limitations: SIGReg, recently proposed in the LeJEPA framework, is incorporated into the VAE training objective to promote a compact, hole-free isotropic Gaussian latent geometry that supports reliable Euclidean deviation scoring; L0 regularization via learnable Hard Concrete gates enforces latent sparsity, deactivating uninformative dimensions and improving interpretability; and an uncertainty-aware W-score is derived from the encoder’s per-dimension means and variances — grounding deviation estimates in aleatoric uncertainty. The combination of SIGReg and L0 regularization is particularly well motivated, as a compact and sparse latent space is a prerequisite for distance-based deviation measures to be geometrically meaningful;
**Interpretability of latent dimensions. ** By independently perturbing each latent dimension and observing the resulting changes in reconstructed anatomy, the authors enable visualization of the anatomical subsystem that each W-score dimension represents. It is a meaningful differentiator from most VAE-based normative modeling works in the literature, which typically treat the latent space as a black box;
**Robust and thorough validation pipeline. ** The authors employ a rigorous evaluation strategy across multiple axes. In- and out-of-distribution test sets are assessed separately, isolating performance on the same-site data from generalization to held-out scanner data. The model’s conditioning on age and sex is appropriately reflected in the evaluation, preventing demographic variation from confounding deviation scores. Finally, W-score Q-Q plots demonstrate that the learned scores follow a normal distribution centered at zero with near-unit variance across healthy subjects, providing empirical support for the Euclidean scoring assumption that underlies the deviation metric.

