reviews.text_contribution:
(1) A dual-phase CT prediction framework with decoupling of temporal variation characteristics was proposed;
(2) A time calibration mechanism based on scan intervals was introduced; 
(3) Two-center validation was conducted, demonstrating favorable external test performance.

reviews.text_weaknesses:
(1) Figure 1 lacks aesthetic appeal and clarity in presentation; 
(2) The formulas and variables in the text fail to specify variable dimensions and domains, hindering formula comprehension; 
(3) The paper only reports AUC and ACC, which are insufficient for medical prediction tasks. Additional metrics such as SEN, SPE, 95% CI, and Delong test should be included; 
(4) Reference errors exist, e.g.: “Treatment responses were categorized per RECIST 1.1 criteria, grouping Complete Response (CR) and Partial Response (PR) as Responders, while Stable Disease (SD) and Progressive Disease (PD) were classified as Non-responders [19].” Reference [19] does not adhere to RECIST 1.1 criteria.
(5) The clinical modeling is insufficient, lacking detailed definitions of key data parameters. For example: Is the CT input the entire thoracic volume, tumor patches, or ROI crops? Does the tumor require manual segmentation or automatic detection? How should patients with multiple lesions be handled? These issues are not clearly addressed.

reviews.text_detailed_comments:
N/A

(1) Lack of critical experimental details, affecting reproducibility and credibility; 
(2) Insufficient statistical validation; 
(3) Incorrect references; 
(4) Two-center external tests were conducted, with significant improvement observed in external testing.

